Q: How can I track the number of unfulfilled promises in a NodeJS application?
A: <p><code>.finally(() =&gt; { console.log('timeout done') })</code> always gets called, unless you never <code>reject</code> that <code>Promise</code>, which happens because you're clearing the timeout apparently when <code>Promise.race</code> is settled.</p>

<p>In your code you assign the timeout id to <code>timer</code> but then issue <code>clearTimeout</code> with <code>timeout</code> variable, which isn't defined in that script. So I'm assuming you're clearing the <code>p2</code> promise timeout, and since <code>p1</code> finishes first, when you clear the timer <code>p2</code> never gets settled.</p>

<p>So don't use <code>clearTimeout</code> since doesn't make sense if you're using <code>Promise.race</code>, and <code>finally</code> will always be called on <code>p2</code></p>

<hr>

<p><strong>Just for debugging purposes</strong>, you can override <code>Promise</code> constructor to keep track of all promises.</p>

<pre><code>Promise = class extends Promise {
    static list = []
    constructor(...args) {
        let p = super(...args)
        Promise.list.push(p)
        return p;
    }
};

// Promise.list (will contain all promises, filter by `pending`
</code></pre>

<p>You can also check this answer: <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/questions/39273680/view-all-pending-promises-in-javascript">View all pending promises in javascript</a></p>

